diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — that neither the 10.5.0.x (VLAN5) side nor the 10.6.0.x (VLAN6) side can reach their gateways (10.5.0.254 and 10.6.0.254) — is not present in the live network. A full diagnostic sweep across all four phases found no fault anywhere in the topology.

**Affected component:** None. Both Layer 3 gateway interfaces (`br_v5` and `br_v6`) on `sshswitch` are operational.

**Mechanism (why the claim is wrong):** The user asserted that after a reload, the "big switch" failed to re-enable its VLAN SVIs. Direct inspection of `sshswitch` shows both bridge interfaces are currently `UP,LOWER_UP` with their correct IPs (`10.5.0.254/24` on `br_v5`, `10.6.0.254/24` on `br_v6`). Live pings from pc1 (10.5.0.1) and pc3 (10.6.0.3) to their respective gateways return 0% loss with sub-millisecond RTT. The hosts' routing tables also confirm they already use these exact addresses as their default gateways.

**Localization:** sshswitch is the multilayer switch acting as VLAN5 and VLAN6 gateway. The OSPF fabric is healthy across all 9 routers (18 routes, all Full adjacencies). No ACL drops, no MAC conflicts, no link faults, no resource pressure detected on any of the 28 devices scanned.

**Root cause:** No fault exists; the gateways came back online correctly after the reload and are fully functional.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required. The network is operating normally.
